{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that captures the explosive rise of facebook and the turbulent personal and legal battles that accompanied it. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie is a riveting blend of sharp dialogue, meticulous pacing, and atmospheric tension that transforms a story about code and startups into a modern tragedy about ambition, betrayal, and isolation.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as a brilliant but emotionally stunted prodigy whose social awkwardness masks a fierce drive to be recognized and validated. his performance is unnervingly precise, capturing the quiet intensity and simmering resentment that fuel zuckerberg’s actions. the supporting cast is equally compelling: andrew garfield as eduardo saverin, the loyal friend turned adversary, brings heartbreak and nuance to his role, while jesse plemons and armie hammer as the winklevoss twins are both eerily identical and strikingly distinct, embodying the entitled privilege that contrasts sharply with zuckerberg’s outsider status.\n\naaron sorkin’s screenplay is a marvel of rhythm and wit. every line feels purposeful, every exchange charged with subtext. the courtroom scenes, framed as flashbacks, are not just narrative devices—they’re dramatic engines that propel the story forward with relentless momentum. the dialogue crackles with intelligence and irony, making even technical discussions about website functionality feel thrilling.\n\ndavid fincher’s direction is cold, clean, and precise, mirroring the digital world the film explores. the cinematography by jeff cronenweth uses a muted palette of grays and blues, reinforcing the emotional detachment of the characters. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross is haunting and electronic, a perfect sonic representation of the digital age’s pulse—ominous, seductive, and lonely.\n\nwhat elevates the film beyond a simple biopic is its thematic depth. it’s not just about facebook—it’s about the cost of innovation, the erosion of friendship in the face of success, and the paradox of connection in an age of isolation. the film asks whether zuckerberg built a platform that connected the world or merely a mirror that reflected his own alienation.\n\nthe social network doesn’t offer easy answers or moral judgments. it presents its characters with unflinching honesty, leaving the audience to grapple with the ambiguity of genius, greed, and guilt. by the end, you’re left not with a sense of triumph, but with a quiet unease—about the people who shape our digital lives, and the price we pay for the connections they offer.\n\nit’s a film that feels more relevant with each passing year, a chillingly accurate portrait of how technology, ambition, and human frailty collide in the 21st century. a modern classic.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0005539313424378633}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.3604399859905243}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.3138180375099182}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0009132028790190816}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.004028658382594585}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -7.295342220459133e-05}, {"token": " captures", "logprob": -0.7201425433158875}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.3470558769768104e-05}, {"token": " explosive", "logprob": -1.5456703901290894}, {"token": " rise", "logprob": -0.0898457020521164}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -5.1973900554003194e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0005115154199302197}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.22678309679031372}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -2.13382354559144e-05}, {"tok